WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.180
- It was one of the hardest
calls that I've had to make

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.930
in the 18 years of running the company.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.340
- [Narrator] Company-wide announcements

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.000
from tech CEOs have all
had similarities recently.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.620
- This is a very difficult decision.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.640
- Tough decision.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.530
- [Narrator] Amazon is
preparing to lay off up

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.390
to 10,000 workers.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:21.210
Meta is cutting 11,000.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:26.210
Netflix has made big cuts, so
has Shopify, Snap, Twitter.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:29.230
Well, maybe that's a unique circumstance.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:30.210
(Elon chuckling)

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.790
Here are the macro and micro reasons

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:35.883
why the tech industry is seeing
massive layoffs right now.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.840
Picture it, Silicon Valley, 2019.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.970
These three major tech companies' revenues

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.180
were growing steadily as
everything moved online.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.150
And their number of
employees was also growing.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.700
Then, of course, the pandemic hit.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.136
People began shopping
from home, binge watching,

00:00:54.136 --> 00:00:55.560
(Netflix starting music)

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.290
and just generally being online more.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:59.640
Not only did the revenues

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.550
of many of these tech companies skyrocket,

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.070
but so did their head count.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.500
- I think Amazon is the
quintessential example here.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.330
If you're promising two-day shipping,

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.220
you have to deliver two-day shipping.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.360
Amazon, in response,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.390
just had to hire many, many more people.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.420
- [Narrator] Not only did Amazon
grow its delivery operation

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.510
but it also grew from its Web Services.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:22.860
- Companies and businesses

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.680
that might not have been online before,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.120
now had an online presence.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.190
You have to host that data somewhere,

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:33.390
and so Amazon saw their cloud
computing service do very well

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.710
during the pandemic.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.780
- [Narrator] Revenues
stayed high through 2021

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.378
and many tech CEOs thought
the growth would continue

00:01:39.378 --> 00:01:43.290
at the same rate they had
seen for the last two years.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.350
- We're gonna continue
to invest aggressively

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.750
into the growth opportunities that we see.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.940
- [Narrator] So they
continued the hiring spree,

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.040
and not just a lot of employees

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.740
but some expensive employees

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.680
because this is also when
the talent wars ramped up

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.710
where companies were vying
for in-demand employees.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.680
Facebook launching the
Metaverse is a prime example.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.500
- From now on, we're going
to be Metaverse-first,

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.910
not Facebook-first.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:12.990
- We saw about 100 or so people
leave Microsoft for Meta,

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:16.380
and some of these people
who left the company said

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.870
that they were being
offered double their salary.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.180
- [Narrator] But by 2022, consumer habits

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.310
had changed in a big way.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:25.740
People were no longer stuck inside.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.800
Netflix saw a drop in
subscribers for the first time

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.000
in over a decade.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.060
People began spending more
of their money on services

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.040
and less on goods.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.680
Revenues for many tech
companies started to stall

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.500
or even decline for the first time.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.998
- Some of these CEOs sort

00:02:41.998 --> 00:02:46.410
of overestimated where
that new middle ground is.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.090
- [Narrator] Which is
why they're explaining,

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:51.810
while apologizing that they grew too fast.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:55.410
Each tech company has also
faced distinct problems.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.810
A major one, Apple's privacy change,

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.760
which required apps to ask users

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.680
if they wanted to be tracked.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.420
It affected a key component

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.400
of many of these social media companies'

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.770
digital ad-focused business models.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.510
Add in the threat from TikTok

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:12.930
taking away viewership from
other social media platforms.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.760
- When you have all these things happening

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.420
at the same time, I think
that's what really did it

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.370
for these companies and put them in.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.740
- [Narrator] The bigger
companies aren't outliers either.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.930
Layoffs have been happening
across the industry

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:28.500
throughout 2022 but have recently surged.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.110
But even though these
are a lot of layoffs,

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:35.430
the companies are not shrinking
back to pre-pandemic levels.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:36.990
Meta's layoffs will bring the number

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.450
of employees to about here.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.060
Amazon's to about here.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.850
Netflix brought the employee count here.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.130
- To be clear, a lot of these
companies are still growing.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:50.430
It's just that the growth is slowing back

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:54.540
to sort of pre-pandemic
or early pandemic levels.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.490
- [Narrator] CEOs also say the timing

00:03:56.490 --> 00:04:00.270
has to do with the threat
of an economic downturn.

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.070
- Well, there certainly have been a lot

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:05.130
of changes very quickly in
the macroeconomic environment.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.890
- We're in a very rapidly
changing economic environment.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:11.130
- In some ways, these
layoffs are a bit proactive.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.410
I means, some of them have
said we're doing this now

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.050
so that we never have to do this again.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:17.910
- [Narrator] These
layoffs, while concentrated

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.280
in the tech industry, are still a lot.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.290
Tens of thousands of people.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:26.178
- For a lot of people, this
reversal and these layoffs

00:04:26.178 --> 00:04:29.460
are something that
they've never seen before.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:30.360
- [Narrator] The tech industry

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.880
had the exceptionally
pronounced experience

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.100
of growing during the pandemic,

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:38.160
and now while the rest of
the labor market is growing,

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.103
they've having the
exceptional experience of not.

